WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.360
Today I wanna talk to you
about a book that has real

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.710
application for language learning.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.010
You know that I'm going to
Sweden, Denmark, Poland.

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:10.470
In preparation, I've been
listening to audiobooks and

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.780
reading eBooks on LingQ, of course.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:18.660
And I discovered this wonderful
Danish, um, audiobook ebook from the,

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.510
uh, online publisher called Saxo.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.650
And the book is, is called
lev mere tænk mindre.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:31.965
Pardon my Danish pronunciation, but
it means live more and think less.

00:00:32.805 --> 00:00:38.114
And, uh, it's by Pia Callesen,
and she talks about what is called

00:00:38.525 --> 00:00:44.295
metacognitive therapy, helping
people who have, you know, stress

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.575
and feel depressed and so forth.

00:00:46.905 --> 00:00:50.864
Now, I'm not suggesting that language
learners are depressed, but one of the

00:00:50.864 --> 00:00:55.770
symptoms of this depression that a lot
of people face, Is this sort of fear,

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.890
call it the social anxiety disorder.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.590
Uh, one of the many kinds of, of negative
feelings that people can develop.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.310
And she explains why people develop
them and how to deal with it.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.020
So it's explained in the book
that typically we are bombarded

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.550
by thoughts all day long.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.010
70,000 thoughts she says, okay.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:23.430
Many of those thoughts are negative and
people who struggle with depression,

00:01:23.804 --> 00:01:26.895
it's not because they have more
negative thoughts than other people,

00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:31.155
but it's because they spend too much
time with their negative thoughts.

00:01:31.304 --> 00:01:33.494
They ruminate, they cogitate.

00:01:34.035 --> 00:01:37.695
So again, it's explained in the book
- and there's also an English language

00:01:37.699 --> 00:01:42.604
version of the book and it is based on
this, uh, metacognitive therapy, which

00:01:42.604 --> 00:01:48.255
came out of the University of Manchester
in England - and she says, so there are

00:01:48.255 --> 00:01:51.104
sort of three levels of our thinking.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.590
At the lowest level are all of these
thoughts that come at us, many of which

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:59.430
might be doubts and negative thoughts
and so forth, and tens of thousands.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:00.420
We all get them.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.420
At the next level is
how we deal with them.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.440
So some people spend a lot of
time delving into these negative

00:02:07.445 --> 00:02:09.390
thoughts, wondering, how did I do?

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:12.110
Uh, does he or she like me?

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:13.740
Am I gonna be fired?

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.100
Uh, is my pronunciation in English bad?

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.350
You know, some of the
language learning anxieties.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.980
People are gonna judge my
ability and the language.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:27.810
All these negative thoughts, we can sort
of dwell on them, which only makes us more

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.660
unhappy or we can choose to ignore them.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:37.019
And she points out in the book that
the sort of key to having a strategy of

00:02:37.025 --> 00:02:42.930
ignoring these negative thoughts, which
she calls trigger thoughts, which can

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.420
trigger sort of anxiety or unhappiness.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.480
The key is to have a belief system.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.920
The belief system will determine
the strategy that we use to

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.600
deal with all of these thoughts.

00:02:55.109 --> 00:03:01.530
So if we believe that dwelling on these
thoughts, thinking about them, uh, is

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:02.910
gonna help us, then that's what we do.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:07.440
Uh, if we think that these thoughts
are outside of our control, or at

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.280
least the negative impact of these
thoughts are outside of our control.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.329
If we think that there's, it's a
function of, say, a, in the case of

00:03:15.329 --> 00:03:19.530
people with, uh, depression, that
is because of a negative imbalance,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.690
then they might go for medication.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:28.829
Or if they think that a psychologist
can analyze these thoughts and find out

00:03:28.829 --> 00:03:32.579
the reasons why we have these negative
thoughts, and in other words, continue

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:36.750
to dwell on them, if we think that's the
right strategy, then that's what we'll do.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.090
And in many cases, we'll be very unhappy.

00:03:39.495 --> 00:03:43.575
If on the other hand, the belief system
is that these thoughts are within our

00:03:43.575 --> 00:03:46.635
control, we can choose to ignore them.

00:03:47.325 --> 00:03:50.954
We can choose to only deal with
them at a certain time of the day.

00:03:51.524 --> 00:03:55.424
Uh, we can choose to address
our focus somewhere else and not

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:57.555
focus on these negative thoughts.

00:03:57.875 --> 00:04:00.915
And if we feel that that's the
right strategy, if that's our

00:04:00.915 --> 00:04:05.655
belief system, then we will adopt
a strategy, a positive strategy for

00:04:05.655 --> 00:04:07.725
dealing with these negative thoughts.

00:04:08.415 --> 00:04:13.335
So I, I think this has real application
for language learning, because very

00:04:13.335 --> 00:04:17.385
often, yeah, we wanna be happy that we're
learning the language, but we want to be

00:04:17.385 --> 00:04:22.395
focused on the enjoyment of the language,
of feeling that moment when we're with

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:24.765
the language where it's an outward focus.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:28.875
It's a focus on the language, on
the audio, the, the language that

00:04:28.875 --> 00:04:32.055
we're listening to, or that we're
reading or we're speaking to people.

00:04:32.175 --> 00:04:36.915
And so it's outward, not inward,
not how did I do, how am I doing?

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:38.550
You know, is this right?

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:39.240
Is that right?

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.260
Why do I make mistakes?

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:41.160
Why do I forget?

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:49.170
So if our sort of mindfulness around our
language learning is one of enjoying what

00:04:49.175 --> 00:04:54.360
we're doing in an outward facing way,
then that's gonna be positive for us.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:58.860
If on the other hand, we somehow
feel that we have to indulge all

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.720
these doubts and neg negative
thoughts, that's probably going to...

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.500
won't drive us into depression, but
it will hinder our language learning.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.010
And so I just, whenever I read
something on any subject, I always

00:05:11.015 --> 00:05:12.480
bring it back to language learning.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:16.080
And I just feel that that is
a, is a discipline and that

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.070
sort of is an understanding.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:21.480
What are our beliefs in terms of how
we deal with these negative thoughts

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:25.965
that we are going to get negative
thoughts about our language and our use

00:05:25.965 --> 00:05:27.344
of the language, we're gonna get them.

00:05:27.495 --> 00:05:28.455
How do we deal with them?

00:05:28.664 --> 00:05:32.534
That's determined by that
higher level, our belief system.

00:05:32.835 --> 00:05:36.045
If we have a belief system that
says the mistakes don't matter,

00:05:36.645 --> 00:05:37.844
we don't need to dwell on them.

00:05:37.905 --> 00:05:40.875
We don't need to try to
remember, you know, how did I do?

00:05:40.875 --> 00:05:42.255
Why did I make a mistake?

00:05:42.585 --> 00:05:43.135
None of that matters.

00:05:43.685 --> 00:05:48.375
It's going forward, enjoying the
language, appreciating what we have

00:05:48.375 --> 00:05:52.515
done and where there are negative
thoughts, which there will be.

00:05:52.515 --> 00:05:53.685
Everyone has them.

00:05:54.105 --> 00:05:57.315
We just ignore them or
detach ourselves from them.

00:05:57.585 --> 00:06:02.325
So I just thought that was a very
interesting, uh, book with real

00:06:02.325 --> 00:06:03.855
application for language learning.

00:06:04.155 --> 00:06:08.025
Check out the link in English probably,
although if you're trying to learn Danish,

00:06:08.025 --> 00:06:09.585
you might wanna check out the Danish book.

00:06:09.825 --> 00:06:10.605
Thank you for listening.

00:06:10.905 --> 00:06:11.355
Bye for now.

